  title="The recursion with the entropy of the small moduli put in: the ends move by 1 to 2% and the ratio turns down",
  gist="In the research session's recursion the class chosen at a modulus s holds e^(v·t) times the mean number of alive rows, which exists only if the smaller moduli have arranged it, and that costs entropy. Summed over a prefix of the moduli the entropy cannot be negative, but in the recursion it is (down to −8 nats at 89 moduli and −219 at 1,228). Requiring every prefix to have entropy ≥ 0 (a convex minimisation: the same recursion with weights w ≥ 1) freezes the small moduli, whose classes then agree in nearly all complete covers: the families of the long runs. The predicted end of complete covers moves by 1 to 2%, and D/(x ln² x) peaks near 1.0 at about a thousand moduli and turns down.",
  detail="D/(x ln² x) with the constraint, against the recursion as first written: 0.912 (x = 463), 0.929 (797), 0.944 (997), 0.975 (1,499), 0.975 (1,999), 0.988 (2,999), 1.008 (9,973), 0.995 (30,000), 0.982 (100,000), 0.960 (300,000), 0.936 (10⁶), that is 89 to 78,497 moduli, against 0.913, 0.933, 0.952, 0.991, 0.995, 1.019, 1.074, 1.097, 1.125, 1.140, 1.154. Found or counted: 0.912 found at 89 moduli, 0.923 to 0.926 at 138 (counts extended), 0.876 found at 167, 0.970 to 0.973 at 238 and at least 0.956 at 302 (counts extended). The last three columns were computed by a contractor session with the same script. The test left open on 24 Nov (1,228 moduli, Q = 300,000): 3,452 with the constraint and 3,409 without, against runs that read 3,465 at 150,000 sweeps and 3,438 at 500,000, still falling; the README says this decides nothing between the two. The figures move by about 1% with the cut of the dispersion at the moduli 3 to 17 (the end on 89 moduli is 15,907, 16,063 or 16,218 for cuts 10⁻⁴, 0.02 and 0.06, against 15,907 found), so the constrained recursion is a 1 to 2% statement about where complete covers end, and an exact treatment of the frozen levels is missing. In the limit the moduli up to Q^a (a < 1) need more entropy than they have, so all but those within a bounded factor of x are frozen and the top moduli must strike all the rows these leave, 0.7413·π(Q)/ln x, from classes that hold O(1) of them: D(n) ≤ (e^γ/(2C₂) + o(1))·r ln² r/ln ln r, and greedy on the top reaches the same order. That part is a heuristic of one day. The consultant's first check confirmed it and the research session recorded that at about 11:20 UTC; the confirmation was withdrawn at about 11:30 UTC: the Lean session's referee read shows that the small moduli can be set to leave far fewer rows than random classes (box ‘A structured prefix beats random classes’), so the bound with 1/ln ln r is not derived. The turn of the table is the statement about typical covers. A direct test of the freezing (the sampled complete covers are counted setting by setting): at 167 moduli the 41 smallest moduli (up to 181), which have 165 nats of classes to choose from, occur in 6 different settings among 1.1 million sampled complete covers at Q = 40,111 (1.1 nats of entropy, where the recursion as it was needs −42 and the constrained one has 0.0), in 21 settings among 1.4 million at Q = 38,000 (2.6 nats), and in 40,481 settings at Q = 25,000, where the constraint does not yet bind. The freezing sets in where the constraint starts to bind and reaches as far up the moduli as the constrained recursion says (16 settings of the first 61 moduli at 40,111, thousands of the first 90); beyond the frozen part the measured entropy grows more slowly than the recursion's, and among the first dozen moduli the recursion spreads the entropy wrongly. One start in one family. A cross-check of the consultant's measurement, from the constrained solution at the end of complete covers (research session, 11:56 UTC): the rows alive after the moduli up to x/ln x, as a share of what random classes leave on the same moduli, are 0.741 at x = 9,973 and 0.772 at x = 1,999, against 0.72 to 0.74 for greedy classes on real primes, and not the 0.36 that the entropy cap would have allowed. In the solution the frozen moduli do what greedy does (a frozen level takes its largest class); the difference between its end (1.0·x ln² x) and greedy's (0.71) is made above x/ln x, by the moduli from 0.1x to 0.4x (still frozen, weights near 1) and by the top half, where the alive rows fall from 0.29 of random at x/2 to nothing. Whether real covers can do that at this size cannot be checked at 1,228 moduli; at 89 moduli the recursion with the Lean session's exact dispersions puts the end 5% too far, which the research session reads as the size of the overstatement.",
