  gist="The research session withdrew its limit 1.1275 on 25 Nov, putting order r ln² r/ln ln r in its place, and asked for an attack on that argument; this is the consultant's check. Split the moduli into small ones (up to x/ω) and top ones. If the small ones leave what random classes leave, 2C₂e^(−γ)·π(Q)/ln x rows, a complete cover needs the largest classes of the top primes to add up to that; with less than one alive row per class near x the largest class holds only ln x/(ln ln x − 1) rows, so such covers end at about 1.349·x ln² x/(ln ln x − 1). The premise fails for structured small moduli (next box), so this is not a bound on D(x), and the verdict that D/(x ln² x) tends to 0 was withdrawn the same day.",
